      "text": "**Re-Evaluating User Input**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the repeated \"OFFLINE\" message. Given the user\u0027s \"Same\" input, it\u0027s clear the LCD isn\u0027t responding, even after I2C interception attempts. I am now assuming the problem is not with the interception, but the lcd itself, or something the interception may have corrupted.\n\n\n**Investigating Core Library Conflict**\n\nI\u0027m now digging into a potential conflict between the bare-metal memory sniffing and the Arduino core\u0027s `Wire` library. Specifically, if `AVRTWI` hooks the same memory addresses as my intercept, I might be disrupting the LCD\u0027s initialization. I need to understand how the `Wire` library blocks the process, and whether Wokwi\u0027s cloud compiler is using a standard `LiquidCrystal_I2C` or something else.\n\n\n**Refining I2C Simulation Strategy**\n\nI\u0027ve hit a potential breakthrough. Rather than assuming a general I2C issue, I am focusing on potential ACK signaling problems within `avr8js`. I\u0027m going to revisit my hook implementation, and confirm that returning `true` (ACK) is, in fact, the correct behavior for `twi.onStart` and `twi.onWrite` within the `avr8js` environment. I am hoping the fix is as simple as tweaking the return values to match the simulator\u0027s expectations. I will also be looking into whether there is a way to look at how the I2C wires are set from the simulation side.\n\n\n**Emulating Serial Communication**\n\nI\u0027ve decided to temporarily bypass the I2C interface altogether. My current plan is to emulate a Serial connection to see if any data can be successfully transmitted, providing a baseline test. This also raises the question: does `LiquidCrystal_I2C` perform checks on the PCF8574 pins during initialization? I am confident in the address.\n\n\n**Correcting Address Calculation**\n\nI just realized a critical error in my I2C address handling. I was shifting the 8-bit address right by one, assuming `avr8js` sent the 8-bit address, when it actually provides the 7-bit address directly to the `onStart` handler. This meant I was using the wrong address (0x13 instead of 0x27)! I\u0027m now updating the `onStart` function to correctly capture the 7-bit address as is. I have removed the now-unnecessary memory sniffer to keep things simpler.\n\n\n**Mapping LCD State**\n\nMy attention has shifted to the `avr8js` v1.0 API, specifically `onStart`, `onWrite`, `onRead`, and `onStop`. I\u0027m confirming correct C++ code, and that the `LiquidCrystal_I2C` library\u0027s pin mapping aligns with the PCF8574 backpack. Currently, RS\u003d0, RW\u003d1, EN\u003d2, and Backlight is 3, while D4-D7 are on bits 4-7. I need to make sure the LCD is in the right state, and that React\u0027s DOM isn\u0027t messing with the ID\u0027s. I am confident that it will not.\n\n\n**Optimizing Rendering Logic**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on efficiently updating the React component, given the high frequency of LCD state changes in the `avr8js` environment. I\u0027ve abandoned `useState` for frequent updates and have implemented a `useRef` to store LCD data, coupled with a lightweight state variable (`lcdRenderCounter`) to trigger re-renders periodically. This approach should prevent React from being overwhelmed by frequent state updates. I will also be testing the display\u0027s online status.\n\n\n**Analyzing LCD State Variables**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the internal state variables I\u0027ve created: `lcdLastEn`, `lcd4BitMode`, `lcdHighNibble`, `lcdLines`, `lcdCursorCol`, and `lcdCursorRow`. I\u0027m starting by analyzing the I2C writes to update the display. I\u0027m focusing on how `rs`, `en`, and the high nibble of data are used to update the LCD display using these variables. The first task is to translate incoming bytes into screen output.\n\n\n**Decoding LCD Commands**\n\nI\u0027m now interpreting the incoming I2C data stream. I see the library sending `0x30` three times, followed by `0x20` to switch to 4-bit mode. If I read `0x20`, it sets `lcd4BitMode` to true, and resets `lcdHighNibble`. If `lcdHighNibble` is null, I store the data as the high nibble. Otherwise, I combine it with the previous high nibble. When `rs` is 0 (command), I see `0x01` is clear, and `0x02` is home. If its greater than `0x80`, its a set cursor position.\n\n\n**Refining Screen Rendering**\n\nI\u0027m now diving deep into how I\u0027m updating the LCD lines. I\u0027m focusing on the `lcdCursorCol` and `lcdCursorRow` variables, along with interpreting data based on `rs`, to ensure characters are correctly written to the display buffer, including whitespace and out-of-bounds checks. I\u0027m now also considering edge cases, such as handling character codes that are less than 0x20. I must be precise.\n\n\n**Confirming Data Integrity**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on guaranteeing the integrity of my I2C data. I\u0027ve confirmed that the rendering updates in `executeFrame` directly manipulate the DOM. This bypasses React\u0027s virtual DOM reconciliation. I\u0027m focusing on confirming the use of `\u003cdiv id\u003d\"lcd-line-0\" /\u003e`, to prevent conflicts.\n\n\n**Implementing I2C Logging**\n\nI\u0027m now intercepting the I2C `console.log` messages to provide visible feedback. I am logging the I2C start address to `sysLogs`. I\u0027ve added a check to only log the I2C address once, to avoid flooding the log. I am now confident that I can see the CPU\u0027s transmissions with these updates.\n\n\n\n**Revisiting I2C Logic**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on confirming correct ACK signaling, given my prior confusion. I\u0027m going to ensure `onStart` and `onWrite` return `true` to ensure ACK. I am also now focused on ensuring `LiquidCrystal_I2C` is properly initialized, and removing my previous test write. I have decided to completely rethink the TWI/I2C from scratch.\n\n\n**Implementing Dual I2C Hooks**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing both the modern and legacy `avr8js` I2C hooks to ensure compatibility. The new approach will have both `onStart` and `onByte` handlers to determine the active address, and should work with either architecture version. This should provide better compatibility across different environments.\n\n\n**Verifying DOM Synchronization**\n\nI\u0027m now carefully checking the DOM synchronization within the React JSX. The offline and online LCD states are properly defined and managed, with appropriate IDs. React will mount the divs empty. My `executeFrame` function will be used to populate `textContent`. React will leave the `textContent` alone.\n\n\\n\\n**Adding I2C Logging**\n\nI\u0027ve implemented a logging mechanism to monitor all I2C traffic, which will confirm online status. If I2C communication occurs, I log the LCD\u0027s online status and address to the serial console for immediate user visibility. This should clarify whether there is any I2C traffic. I\u0027m also confirming what is happening with the memory sniffer.\n\n\n**Validating I2C Communication**\n\nI am now trusting `twi.onStart` and `twi.onWrite` to manage I2C, given the evidence. It\u0027s clear that the LCD did initialize, as the user reported the full startup sequence, so I will now focus on using the native hooks correctly, rather than trying to directly intercept memory writes. This will remove the need for the memory sniffer, which may have been interfering with the process.\n\n\n**Verifying Native Hooks**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing `onStart(addr)` and `onWrite(byte)` using the `AVRTWI` hooks, replacing the memory sniffer with a more direct approach.  I\u0027m using logging to confirm I2C activity and the online state. I\u0027m checking the hardware TWI usage. I have removed the now-unnecessary memory sniffer.\n\n\n**Confirming Line Updates**\n\nI\u0027m now investigating potential discrepancies between LCD state updates and DOM updates, specifically related to line IDs. I\u0027m double-checking the DOM structure, paying particular attention to `\u003cdiv id\u003d\"lcd-line-0-${disp.id}\" /\u003e`, to make certain that the render loop targets the correct elements within `fastUpdateDOM()`. I\u0027ve also identified an issue where the online status was hiding the online display, but the issue has been resolved.\n\n\n**Analyzing Tailwind Overrides**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on a Tailwind CSS conflict. I realize that Tailwind\u0027s `hidden` class uses `display: none !important`, which may take precedence over inline styles. While inline styles *should* override it, I\u0027m switching to using `classList.remove(\u0027hidden\u0027)` and `classList.add(\u0027hidden\u0027)` to directly manipulate the classes, eliminating any potential conflicts and ensuring precise control over the display state.\n\n\n**Confirming Byte Interpretation**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on the byte-level interpretation of I2C data. I am double-checking that my bitwise operations accurately extract `rs`, `en`, and the nibbles for data transfer. I will also make sure the data is interpreted as expected. I need to ensure that the correct data is extracted from the bytes coming from the I2C to be rendered on the DOM display.\n\n\n**Validating Bitwise Operations**\n\nI\u0027m now meticulously reviewing my bitwise operations for extracting LCD control signals and data from the I2C bytes, and confirming my understanding of the 4-bit initialization sequence. My code for the falling edge handling of the EN signal looks correct, but I have a strong hunch I can refactor it for better clarity. I am now working on confirming my code can translate the bits coming in to text.\n\n\n**Refining Initialization Sequence**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the HD44780 initialization sequence, realizing that premature 4-bit mode activation was the cause of desynchronization. I will refine the logic, and I will be more precise in handling the 0x20 command that sets it to 4-bit mode. The existing method caused misinterpretation of the I2C data stream.\n\n\n**Refining Mode Detection**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on an initialization issue, and I suspect that early 4-bit mode activation caused desynchronization. I will refine the logic, focusing on the 0x20 command that sets it to 4-bit mode. I will also make the decoder more resilient, by resetting if `lcdHighNibble` is populated for too long.\n\n\n**Optimizing Rendering Strategy**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing a new rendering strategy for the LCD display. Instead of relying on frequent state updates, I\u0027m now pushing strings to a `useRef` for the text nodes. The `fastUpdateDOM()` function will use opacity for display visibility. This approach bypasses Tailwind conflicts and provides better performance, especially when using a direct DOM.\n\n\n**Verifying C++ Code**\n\nI\u0027m now cross-referencing my simulated I2C environment with the provided C++ code. I\u0027m verifying the `LiquidCrystal_I2C` library\u0027s initialization and the use of the I2C address, along with debugging with logs. I\u0027m confirming that my logging is working correctly and the log messages are not being overwhelmed by characters. I\u0027ve re-reviewed the I2C signatures.\n\n\n**Confirming I2C Address**\n\nI\u0027ve confirmed that the `avr8js` version within the Wokwi environment may be outdated, so I\u0027m now hooking both the legacy `onByte` and the modern `onStart` handlers, along with adding an address flag. I\u0027m prioritizing `onStart` to identify if the current API is in use, which will determine the address. I am also planning to log the result to ensure its accuracy.\n\n\n**Refining Address Detection**\n\nI\u0027m now refining the address detection logic to work with older `avr8js` versions. I\u0027ve re-reviewed the I2C signatures. It seems the old API just has `onByte`, which means it can be harder to detect the start of the I2C transmission. The current idea is to test for this with a new `i2cAddress` flag. I am also working on testing the I2C signatures.\n\n\n**Logging I2C Byte Data**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing detailed I2C logging for the initial bytes to understand the data stream. I will use a `logBuffer.push` and `logI2C()` function to monitor the first ten bytes from `onByte` and `onWrite`. If `LiquidCrystal_I2C` attempts to read the busy flag, `twi.onRead` should return `0xFF`, which I am now ready for.\n\n\n**Verifying Read Response**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing the `onRead` handler to return `0xFF`, as indicated by the LCD data sheet for the busy flag. I\u0027ve re-examined the DOM structure to ensure offline and online states are correctly layered, focusing on element IDs and `opacity` control. I will now confirm the expected behavior of the simulation.\n\n\n**Validating I2C Library**\n\nI am now confirming the `LiquidCrystal_I2C` library\u0027s integration with the `Wire.h` library within the Wokwi environment. Since the LCD is now responding, and I can confirm that `lcd.init()` calls `Wire.begin()` internally, so using `lcd.init()` should be enough. I\u0027m also confirming the integrity of the logging updates.\n\n\n**Analyzing Byte Interpretation**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on decoding the I2C bytes. The key is understanding `rs`, `en`, and the nibbles. I will ensure my bitwise operations accurately extract the correct parts of the data, and update my LCD state variables accordingly, starting with `lcdHighNibble`. I am confident that I can use the edge case for EN signal in the LCD decoding.\n\n\n**Decoding Incoming Data**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on incoming I2C data translation. My logic correctly decodes incoming characters, handles commands like clear and home, and translates cursor set commands into row/column updates. I\u0027m also addressing special characters and out-of-bounds cursor updates to ensure reliable LCD rendering. I will be sure the data coming is proper.\n\n\n**Implementing Nibble Timeout**\n\nI\u0027ve just added a timeout to the LCD decoder. Now, if an odd number of nibbles are sent, causing `lcdHighNibble` to remain, the counter will expire, resetting the process and preventing future corruption. I hope this stabilizes the LCD rendering.\n\n\n**Verifying LCD Address**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing address validation and confirming the fallback `twi.onByte` logic for older `avr8js` versions. Logging raw I2C bytes to the system console will confirm the correct LCD address and data stream. I will use the console for user feedback. I will also be confirming whether online states are valid.\n\n\n**Clarifying System Console Feedback**\n\nI\u0027m now making sure the user is aware of critical system console logs for debugging, especially raw I2C data like \"I2C Raw Byte: 0x4E.\" I\u0027m refining `fastUpdateDOM()` to explicitly target the LCD lines and control opacity for online/offline states.\n\n\n**Refining Online Status**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the online/offline display. I am creating two divs, `lcd-offline` and `lcd-online`, that contain transition opacity. The `lcd-online` div, which will contain `lcd-line` divs, is hidden by default. The `fastUpdateDOM()` function will then populate the content of the `lcd-line` divs.\n\n\n**Confirming Online Display**\n\nI\u0027m now confirming the proper operation of the online/offline display logic. I\u0027m confident that the use of opacity transitions will smoothly transition the display. The `fastUpdateDOM` is now correctly handling the updates, and I\u0027ve eliminated the Tailwind CSS `display` conflict. I am verifying the logic.\n\n\n**Refining Hook Integration**\n\nI\u0027m now integrating both `onByte` and `onStart/onWrite` TWI handlers for full compatibility with all `avr8js` versions. I\u0027m focusing on ensuring these hooks are bound correctly to the `AVRTWI` instance and that they are activated. I\u0027m also now planning to log all incoming bytes to the console.\n\n\n**Finalizing Display Integration**\n\nI am now ready to present a final update. I\u0027ve cross-referenced the provided C++ code. The system console will display all I2C traffic, which allows the user to see exactly what is happening. The user should use the Opacity transition build, and confirm system console logs for debugging.",
